      correctIndex: 2,
      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Property Acquisition: Allocation of Purchase Price</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">$600,000</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">The Answer</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">$600,000 is the correct answer</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Explanation</h3><p style="margin:0">To determine the value of the building, we need to properly allocate the total cost between the land and building based on their relative fair values.</p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f8ff; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:20px"><div style="color:#01506e; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:12px">Step 1: Determine the total cost of the property</div><p style="margin:0">Total cost = Cash paid + Mortgage assumed</p><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Total cost = $750,000 + $250,000 = $1,000,000</strong></div></div><div style="background-color:#f0f8ff; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:20px"><div style="color:#01506e; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:12px">Step 2: Use relative fair values for allocation</div><p style="margin:0">The property is assessed at $960,000, which represents the fair value.</p><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Building&#x27;s proportion = 60% of $960,000 = $576,000</strong></div><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Land&#x27;s proportion = 40% of $960,000 = $384,000</strong></div></div><div style="background-color:#f0f8ff; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:20px"><div style="color:#01506e; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:12px">Step 3: Calculate allocation ratios</div><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Building ratio = $576,000 ÷ $960,000 = 0.6 (60%)</strong></div><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Land ratio = $384,000 ÷ $960,000 = 0.4 (40%)</strong></div></div><div style="background-color:#f0f8ff; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:20px"><div style="color:#01506e; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:12px">Step 4: Apply ratios to actual cost</div><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Building value = 0.6 × $1,000,000 = $600,000</strong></div><div style="background-color:white; border:1px solid #dddddd; border-radius:5px; color:#12232d; font-family:monospace; margin:0 0 12px 0; padding:12px; text-align:center"><strong>Land value = 0.4 × $1,000,000 = $400,000</strong></div></div><div style="background-color:white; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Analysis of Answer Options</h3><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Option</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Analysis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$500,000</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. This amount doesn&#x27;t properly allocate the cost based on the relative fair values.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$576,000</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. This represents 60% of the assessed value ($960,000), not 60% of the actual cost.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$600,000</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Correct. This is 60% of the total cost of $1,000,000, properly allocating the actual purchase price based on relative fair values.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$960,000</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Incorrect. This is the total assessed value, not the allocated cost of the building.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">When acquiring property with multiple components (land and building), the total cost ($1,000,000) must be allocated based on the relative fair values of each component. Since the building represents 60% of the total fair value, it should be recorded at 60% of the total cost, which is $600,000.</p><p style="margin:0">The cost principle requires assets to be recorded at their acquisition cost, not at their fair market value. That&#x27;s why we use the relative fair values to allocate the actual cost paid.</p></div></div></div>`,
